IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `hca-dates`
IMPORT `hca-rules`

-- Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) -- the questions of the COMPLAINANT.
--
-- SEAT: the person who complains to the Health Complaints Commissioner about a health service -- usually the health
-- service user (the patient), or a parent, attorney, carer or other person s 22 lets complain for them, or anyone
-- complaining under s 22A that a health care worker breached a code of conduct. "Is this a health service?", "Can I
-- complain?", "Is what happened a ground of complaint?", "By when must I complain?", "Must my complaint be dismissed?",
-- "By when must the Commissioner assess it and tell me?", "Can it go to conciliation, must I go, can I bring someone?",
-- "Can what I say there be used in court?", "Is our agreement binding?", "Do I get the report?", "Has my provider
-- answered the Commissioner's notice in time?", "Can my identity be kept from the provider?", "Can I be sued for
-- complaining?", "Is what was done to me for complaining an offence?". The health service provider, the health care
-- worker, the Commissioner, a conciliator and a registration board get no seat here: the Commissioner's opinions and
-- satisfactions are asked as facts.
--
-- Every question takes plain facts (yes/no, dates, numbers, a choice from a list) and calls the rules of hca-rules.l4.
-- Four DEONTIC rules (the 2-year time limit, s 25(5)(c); the 14 days after a warning, s 25(7); the written agreement,
-- s 39(2); the provider's answer, s 56(3)) are run inside the questions that need them; no question takes a DEONTIC or
-- an EVENT.
--
-- A complaint here is not a claim for compensation: the Act gives the Commissioner no power to award damages. A claim
-- for medical negligence is made in a court, within the Limitation Act 1974 (Tas) (encoded beside this Act at
-- subjects/au/tas/limitation-act-1974/encodings/baseline-2026-11/); once a court has commenced to hear it, the
-- complaint must be dismissed (s 25(5)(f)), and what is said in conciliation cannot be used in it (s 37).
--
-- UNOFFICIAL. Based on material from the Tasmanian Legislation website at 6 November 2026 (c) State of Tasmania, CC BY
-- 4.0; not the authorised version. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md. BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas): the complainant's questions`

§§ `Can I complain`

@export Is this a "health service" that a complaint to the Tasmanian Health Complaints Commissioner can be about (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) s 3, Schedule 1)? Yes for any service for, or purportedly for, the benefit of human health, including hospital, medical, dental, pharmacy, mental health, aged and disability care, ambulance, allied health, alternative health and health information services, and administrative services directly related to them. No for an opinion or decision for a workers compensation claim or an asbestos compensation application, a service that is not for human health, or a service the regulations exclude.
GIVEN `what kind of service was it` IS A `Kind of service` @desc The kind of service you are complaining about, chosen from the list (Schedule 1 Part 1 lists the services that are included, Part 2 the two that are excluded)
      `do the regulations prescribe it as not a health service` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether regulations under the Act prescribe this service as not being a health service (answer no if you do not know of any)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is this a health service I can complain about` MEANS
    `s 3 -- it is a health service` `what kind of service was it` `do the regulations prescribe it as not a health service`

@export Am I entitled to make a complaint to the Health Complaints Commissioner about this health service (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) ss 22, 22A)? Yes if you are the patient (the health service user); for a patient aged 14 to 17, a person the patient appointed; for a patient under 14, a parent or guardian; an attorney whose power covers complaints; a person who has the care of the patient's affairs under a law or court order; a person the Commissioner approves where the patient cannot complain; a health service provider; the Minister, Health Minister or Health Department Secretary; or anyone else the Commissioner permits. Anyone may complain that a health care worker breached a code of conduct. Otherwise no, and the complaint must be dismissed (s 25(5)(a)).
GIVEN `who am I` IS A `Who I am` @desc Who you are in relation to the patient: the health service user; a person the child appointed to complain for them; a parent or guardian of the child; the donee of a power of attorney from the health service user; a person who has the care of the user's affairs under a law or a court order; a person the Commissioner approved because the user cannot complain; a health service provider; the Minister, the Health Minister or the Secretary of the Health Department; or someone else
      `how old is the patient` IS A NUMBER @desc The patient's (health service user's) age in whole years on the day you make the complaint (the Act does not say when age is tested; fork F11)
      `does the power of attorney cover complaints under this Act` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Only for an attorney: whether the patient's power of attorney gives you authority to make a complaint under the Health Complaints Act
      `is it difficult or impossible for the patient to complain` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is difficult or impossible for the patient to make a complaint, or one in the form the Act asks for
      `has the Commissioner approved me to complain` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner has approved you to complain for the patient
      `has the Commissioner permitted me to complain` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner considers that, in the circumstances, you should be permitted to complain though no other category fits you
      `is my complaint that a health care worker breached a code of conduct` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your complaint is that a health care worker (an unregistered practitioner, or one acting outside their registration) acted in breach of a code of conduct under the Act
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I entitled to make this complaint` MEANS
    `ss 22, 22A -- I am entitled to complain` `who am I` `how old is the patient` `does the power of attorney cover complaints under this Act` (`is it difficult or impossible for the patient to complain`) `has the Commissioner approved me to complain` `has the Commissioner permitted me to complain` `is my complaint that a health care worker breached a code of conduct`

@export Is what I want to complain about a ground of complaint under the Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) (ss 23(1), 25(5)(b))? Yes for: not providing a service unreasonably; an unnecessary service; providing it unreasonably; lack of due skill; unprofessional treatment; disrespect of privacy or dignity; not enough information, choice, advice or prognosis; unreasonably denying access to your records or information about your condition; unreasonable disclosure of your information; not dealing properly with your complaint to the provider; or anything else inconsistent with the Charter of Health Rights. A complaint that discloses none of these must be dismissed.
GIVEN `what is my complaint` IS A `Ground of complaint` @desc What you say happened, chosen from the grounds in s 23(1), or "a health care worker breached a code of conduct, and nothing else", or "something else"
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my complaint about a ground the Act covers` MEANS
    `ss 23(1), 25(5)(b) -- the complaint discloses a ground` `what is my complaint`

@export What form must my complaint take (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) s 23(3)-(5))? A complaint is to be in writing, signed by you, and give your name and address. The Commissioner may accept one that is not; if an oral complaint is accepted, you must put it in writing and sign it if the Commissioner requires (unless the Commissioner is satisfied there is good reason not to).
GIVEN `is it in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your complaint is in writing
      `have I signed it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have signed it
      `does it give my name and address` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it gives your name and address
      `has the Commissioner accepted it orally` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner has accepted your complaint made orally (for example by telephone)
GIVETH A `Form of my complaint`
`What form must my complaint take` MEANS
    `s 23(3)-(5) -- the form of my complaint` `is it in writing` `have I signed it` `does it give my name and address` `has the Commissioner accepted it orally`

§§ `The time limit`

@export By when must I make my complaint to the Health Complaints Commissioner (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) s 25(5)(c), (ca))? Within 2 years after the patient became aware of what gave rise to it; for a complaint about a health care worker, also within 2 years after you became aware, whichever ends first. The answer is the last day. Later, the complaint must be dismissed unless the Commissioner is satisfied you had good reason for the delay (s 25(6)).
GIVEN `when did the patient become aware of what happened` IS A DATE @desc The day the patient (health service user) became aware of the circumstances that gave rise to the complaint
      `is it about a health care worker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the complaint relates to a health care worker (an unregistered practitioner, or one acting outside their registration)
      `when did I become aware of what happened` IS A DATE @desc The day you, the complainant, became aware of those circumstances (the same day if you are the patient)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I make my complaint` MEANS
    `s 25(5)(c), (ca) -- the last day for my complaint` `when did the patient become aware of what happened` `is it about a health care worker` `when did I become aware of what happened`

@export Is my complaint within the 2-year time limit of the Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) (s 25(5)(c), (ca), (6))? Yes if made no more than 2 years after the patient became aware of what gave rise to it (and, for a complaint about a health care worker, no more than 2 years after you did), or if the Commissioner is satisfied you had good reason for not complaining within the 2 years. Otherwise no: the Commissioner must dismiss it.
GIVEN `when did the patient become aware of what happened` IS A DATE @desc The day the patient (health service user) became aware of the circumstances that gave rise to the complaint
      `is it about a health care worker` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the complaint relates to a health care worker (an unregistered practitioner, or one acting outside their registration)
      `when did I become aware of what happened` IS A DATE @desc The day you, the complainant, became aware of those circumstances (the same day if you are the patient)
      `when did I make my complaint` IS A DATE @desc The day you made (or will make) the complaint
      `is the Commissioner satisfied I had good reason for the delay` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner is satisfied you had good reason for not complaining within 2 years of becoming aware
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is my complaint in time` MEANS
    `s 25(5)(c), (ca), (6) -- my complaint is in time` `when did the patient become aware of what happened` `is it about a health care worker` `when did I become aware of what happened` `when did I make my complaint` `is the Commissioner satisfied I had good reason for the delay`

§§ `Assessment and dismissal`

@export Must the Commissioner dismiss my complaint on one of the grounds in s 25(5)(d)-(j) of the Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas)? Yes if the Commissioner is satisfied that: you did not, without good reason, take reasonable steps to sort it out with the provider first; all the issues have been decided by a court, tribunal or board; a court has started hearing a proceeding about it (for example your negligence claim); you have had reasonable explanations and going on would bring no benefit; it lacks substance; it is frivolous, vexatious or not in good faith; or it has been resolved. Otherwise these grounds do not require dismissal (who may complain, the grounds and the time limit are separate questions).
GIVEN `did I fail without good reason to take reasonable steps to resolve it with the provider` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner is satisfied you failed, without good reason, to take reasonable steps to resolve the grievance with the provider
      `have all the issues been adjudicated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner is satisfied all the issues have been decided by a court, tribunal, board or similar body in Australia
      `has a court commenced to hear a proceeding about it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a court has started to hear a proceeding about the subject of the complaint (not just that one has been filed)
      `have I had reasonable explanations so that going on would bring no benefit` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner is satisfied you have been given reasonable explanations and information and there would be no benefit in going on
      `does it lack substance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner is satisfied the complaint lacks substance
      `is it frivolous, vexatious or not in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner is satisfied the complaint is frivolous, vexatious or was not made in good faith
      `has it been resolved` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the complaint has been resolved
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must my complaint be dismissed on another ground` MEANS
    `s 25(5)(d)-(j) -- the Commissioner must dismiss it` `did I fail without good reason to take reasonable steps to resolve it with the provider` `have all the issues been adjudicated` `has a court commenced to hear a proceeding about it` `have I had reasonable explanations so that going on would bring no benefit` `does it lack substance` `is it frivolous, vexatious or not in good faith` `has it been resolved`

@export By when must I meet the Commissioner's requirement for further information after a written warning that my complaint might be dismissed (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) s 25(7))? By the 14th day after the warning notice was provided to you. After that, if the requirement is still not met, the Commissioner may dismiss the complaint.
GIVEN `when was the warning provided to me` IS A DATE @desc The day the Commissioner's written notice, warning that the complaint might be dismissed unless the requirement is met within 14 days, was provided to you
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I meet the requirement after a warning` MEANS
    `s 25(7) -- the last day after the warning` `when was the warning provided to me`

@export Can the Commissioner dismiss my complaint on this day for not meeting a requirement to give further information, documents or a statutory declaration (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) ss 24, 25(7))? Only if the period set for the requirement has ended, the Commissioner then warned you in writing that the complaint might be dismissed unless you met it within 14 days, those 14 days have passed, and you still have not met it.
GIVEN `when did the period set for the requirement end` IS A DATE @desc The last day of the period the Commissioner set for meeting the requirement (as extended, if it was)
      `did the Commissioner warn me in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner has notified you in writing that the complaint might be dismissed unless the requirement is met within 14 days
      `when was the warning provided to me` IS A DATE @desc The day that warning notice was provided to you
      `have I met the requirement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have provided what was required
      `when did I meet it` IS A DATE @desc The day you met the requirement (any day if you have not)
      `the day asked about` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about (for example today)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my complaint be dismissed for not meeting the requirement on this day` MEANS
    `s 25(7) -- the Commissioner may dismiss my complaint on that day` `when did the period set for the requirement end` `did the Commissioner warn me in writing` `when was the warning provided to me` `have I met the requirement` `when did I meet it` `the day asked about`

@export By when must the Commissioner assess my complaint (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) s 25(1), (1C), (1CA))? Within 45 days after receiving it. The Commissioner may extend this by up to 45 more days (after an unsuccessful attempt at early resolution, or if necessary for the assessment), and for as long as necessary if information sought is delayed, and must tell you of any extension. The answer is the last day.
GIVEN `when did the Commissioner receive my complaint` IS A DATE @desc The day the Commissioner received your complaint
      `how many days was the assessment extended for under s 25(1C)` IS A NUMBER @desc Days of extension the Commissioner notified you of under s 25(1C) (0 if none; at most 45 count)
      `how many days was the assessment extended for a delay in information` IS A NUMBER @desc Days of extension the Commissioner notified you of under s 25(1CA), because of a delay in obtaining information (0 if none)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must my complaint be assessed` MEANS
    `s 25(1) -- the last day for assessment` `when did the Commissioner receive my complaint` `how many days was the assessment extended for under s 25(1C)` `how many days was the assessment extended for a delay in information`

@export By when must I be given written notice of the Commissioner's assessment of my complaint (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) s 27)? Within 14 days after the Commissioner makes the determination (to dismiss, refer, conciliate or investigate). The answer is the last day.
GIVEN `when did the Commissioner make the determination` IS A DATE @desc The day the Commissioner made the assessment determination
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I be told of the assessment` MEANS
    `s 27 -- the last day for notice of the assessment` `when did the Commissioner make the determination`

@export If I withdraw my complaint, by when must the health service provider be told (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) s 30)? You may withdraw at any time by written notice to the Commissioner. If the provider had been notified of the complaint, the Commissioner must tell the provider within 14 days; if not, there is no such duty. The Commissioner then takes no further action except an investigation under Part 6, which can go on despite the withdrawal.
GIVEN `had the provider been notified of my complaint` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the health service provider had been notified that your complaint was received
      `when did I give written notice of withdrawal` IS A DATE @desc The day you notified the Commissioner in writing that you withdraw the complaint
GIVETH A MAYBE DATE
`By when must the provider be told I withdrew` MEANS
    `s 30(2)(a) -- the last day to tell the provider` `had the provider been notified of my complaint` `when did I give written notice of withdrawal`

§§ `Conciliation`

-- Arises only if the Commissioner refers the complaint, or part of it, to a conciliator.

@export Can my complaint be referred to conciliation (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) s 25(1A), (4))? Only if the Commissioner thinks it appropriate, and never if it appears to indicate a significant issue of public safety or public interest, or a significant question about the provider's practice, or if the patient's identity has to be kept from the provider (s 64). That the identity of a complainant who is not the patient is protected does not bar it.
GIVEN `does the Commissioner think conciliation appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner is of the opinion that it is appropriate to refer the complaint to a conciliator
      `does it indicate a significant issue of public safety or public interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the complaint appears to the Commissioner to indicate a significant issue of public safety or public interest
      `does it indicate a significant question about the provider's practice` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the complaint appears to the Commissioner to indicate a significant question as to the practice of the provider
      `must the patient's identity be kept from the provider` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner believes on reasonable grounds that disclosing who the patient (the health service user) is to the provider would risk health or safety, a lower standard of service, or the assessment or investigation (s 64). If you are the patient, this is your own identity; the protection of a complainant who is not the patient does not count here (s 25(4)(b))
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can my complaint go to conciliation` MEANS
    `s 25(1A), (4) -- my complaint can go to conciliation` `does the Commissioner think conciliation appropriate` `does it indicate a significant issue of public safety or public interest` `does it indicate a significant question about the provider's practice` `must the patient's identity be kept from the provider`

@export Must I attend this conciliation meeting (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) s 32A)? Yes for the initial meeting once the Commissioner has referred the complaint for conciliation; after that, taking part is voluntary and either party may withdraw at any time.
GIVEN `was my complaint referred for conciliation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner has referred your complaint for conciliation
      `is it the initial meeting` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether this is the initial conciliation meeting
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must I attend this conciliation meeting` MEANS
    `s 32A -- I must attend` `was my complaint referred for conciliation` `is it the initial meeting`

@export Can I be represented by someone else (for example a lawyer or advocate) at conciliation (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) s 33)? Only if the Commissioner is satisfied that representation is likely to assist substantially in resolving the complaint, and you told the other party in writing, at least 48 hours before asking the Commissioner, that you would ask.
GIVEN `is the Commissioner satisfied representation would assist substantially` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner is satisfied that representation is likely to assist substantially in resolving the complaint
      `did I notify the other party in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you notified the other party in writing that you would ask the Commissioner to allow representation
      `on what day did I notify them` IS A DATE @desc The day of that written notice
      `at what hour did I notify them` IS A NUMBER @desc The hour of that notice on the 24-hour clock (for example 14 for 2 pm)
      `on what day did I ask the Commissioner` IS A DATE @desc The day you asked the Commissioner to allow representation
      `at what hour did I ask the Commissioner` IS A NUMBER @desc The hour of that request on the 24-hour clock
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I be represented at conciliation` MEANS
    `s 33 -- I may be represented at conciliation` `is the Commissioner satisfied representation would assist substantially` `did I notify the other party in writing` `on what day did I notify them` `at what hour did I notify them` `on what day did I ask the Commissioner` `at what hour did I ask the Commissioner`

@export Can what was said or admitted in conciliation be used as evidence in court, for example in a negligence claim (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) s 37)? No: anything said or admitted during conciliation is not admissible in a court or tribunal, and the Commissioner cannot use it as a ground for investigation. The Act says nothing about things said outside conciliation.
GIVEN `was it said or admitted during conciliation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the statement or admission was made during the conciliation
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can it be used as evidence in court` MEANS
    `s 37(1) -- it is admissible in court` `was it said or admitted during conciliation`

@export Is the agreement we reached in conciliation binding (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) s 39)? Not unless it is in writing and entered into within 14 days after the verbal agreement was reached in conciliation. If it is, and you both put it in a form binding on you, yes. An agreement reached outside conciliation is left to the general law.
GIVEN `was it reached in the course of conciliation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you and the provider reached the agreement in the course of the conciliation
      `is it in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the agreement has been put in writing
      `when was the verbal agreement reached` IS A DATE @desc The day you reached the agreement verbally in conciliation
      `when was the written agreement entered into` IS A DATE @desc The day the written agreement was entered into (signed)
      `did we put it in a form binding on us` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you both put the agreement in a form meant to bind you (for example a signed deed or agreement)
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is our conciliation agreement binding` MEANS
    `s 39 -- our conciliation agreement is binding` `was it reached in the course of conciliation` `is it in writing` `when was the verbal agreement reached` `when was the written agreement entered into` `did we put it in a form binding on us`

§§ `Investigation`

-- Arises only if the Commissioner investigates under Part 6.

@export Will I be given a copy of the Commissioner's investigation report (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) s 55(2)(f))? Yes if the investigation was of your complaint, or of a significant issue or question arising from it. Not under s 55 for other investigations, including one into a health care worker's possible breach of a code of conduct.
GIVEN `what was investigated` IS A `Kind of investigation` @desc What the Commissioner investigated: an investigation of my complaint; an investigation of an issue or question arising from my complaint; an investigation of a possible breach of a code of conduct by a health care worker; an investigation directed by the Health Minister; or an investigation of another matter on the Commissioner's own motion
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Will I get a copy of the investigation report` MEANS
    `s 55(2)(f) -- I am to be given a copy of the report` `what was investigated`

@export Will I be given the Commissioner's statement of decision on an order against a health care worker (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) s 56AAD(2)(a))? Yes for a prohibition order made as the result of your complaint (confidential material may be removed). Not for an order that did not result from your complaint. The Act does not say for an interim prohibition order.
GIVEN `what order was made` IS A `Order made` @desc The order made against the health care worker: a prohibition order; an interim prohibition order; or no order
      `was it made as the result of my complaint` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the order was made as the result of your complaint
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Will I get the statement of decision on the order` MEANS
    `s 56AAD(2)(a) -- I am to be given the statement of decision` `what order was made` `was it made as the result of my complaint`

@export By when must my health service provider tell the Commissioner what it has done about a notice of recommended action (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) s 56(3), (4))? Within 45 days after receiving the notice, or up to 15 days more if the provider asked in writing before the 45 days ended and the Commissioner extended the period. The answer is the last day.
GIVEN `when did the provider receive the notice` IS A DATE @desc The day the provider received the Commissioner's notice of recommended action
      `did the provider ask in writing for more time before the 45 days ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner received the provider's written request for an extension before the 45 days ended
      `how many days of extension did the Commissioner allow` IS A NUMBER @desc Days of extension the Commissioner allowed (0 if none; at most 15 count)
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must my provider answer the notice` MEANS
    `s 56(3) -- the provider's last day` `when did the provider receive the notice` `did the provider ask in writing for more time before the 45 days ended` `how many days of extension did the Commissioner allow`

@export Has my health service provider answered the Commissioner's notice of recommended action in time (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) s 56(3), (4))? The provider must advise the Commissioner in writing of the action it has taken to remedy your grievances within 45 days after receiving the notice (or the extended period). Failing to is an offence with a fine of up to 50 penalty units, and the Commissioner may name a provider who unreasonably failed to act on the notice in a published report (s 13(4)(b)).
GIVEN `when did the provider receive the notice` IS A DATE @desc The day the provider received the Commissioner's notice of recommended action
      `did the provider ask in writing for more time before the 45 days ended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner received the provider's written request for an extension before the 45 days ended
      `how many days of extension did the Commissioner allow` IS A NUMBER @desc Days of extension the Commissioner allowed (0 if none; at most 15 count)
      `has the provider advised the Commissioner in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provider has advised the Commissioner in writing of the action it has taken
      `when did the provider advise the Commissioner` IS A DATE @desc The day it did so (any day if it has not)
      `the day asked about` IS A DATE @desc The day you are asking about (for example today)
GIVETH A `The provider's answer`
`Has my provider answered the notice in time` MEANS
    `s 56(3) -- the provider's answer` `when did the provider receive the notice` `did the provider ask in writing for more time before the 45 days ended` `how many days of extension did the Commissioner allow` `has the provider advised the Commissioner in writing` `when did the provider advise the Commissioner` `the day asked about`

§§ `Protection for complainants`

@export Must the Commissioner keep my identity (or the patient's) from the health service provider (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) s 64)? Yes if the Commissioner believes on reasonable grounds that disclosing it would put anyone's health or safety at risk, lead to a lower standard of health service for someone, or prejudice the assessment or investigation. A complaint cannot go to conciliation if the patient's (the health service user's) identity must be kept from the provider (s 25(4)(b)); protecting a complainant who is not the patient does not bar it.
GIVEN `does the Commissioner believe disclosure would risk health, safety, care or the process` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Commissioner believes, on reasonable grounds, that disclosing who you or the patient are would put anyone's health or safety at risk, lead to a lower standard of health service, or prejudice the assessment or investigation
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Must my identity be kept from the provider` MEANS
    `s 64(1) -- my identity must be kept from the provider` `does the Commissioner believe disclosure would risk health, safety, care or the process`

@export Am I protected from being sued for making my complaint, or for giving the Commissioner or a conciliator statements, information or documents (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) s 73)? Yes if you acted in good faith: you are not liable for any loss, damage or injury another person suffers as a result. If not in good faith, the Act gives no protection.
GIVEN `did I act in good faith` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you made the complaint, or gave the statement, information or document, in good faith
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I protected from being sued for complaining` MEANS
    `s 73 -- I am protected from liability for complaining` `did I act in good faith`

@export Was it an offence to threaten or intimidate me to stop me complaining, to make me withdraw, or to stop me co-operating or giving information (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) s 67)? Yes, with a fine of up to 100 penalty units, unless the person had a reasonable excuse, or has the defence that they had another, reasonable ground for the conduct.
GIVEN `was I threatened or intimidated over my complaint` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether someone persuaded or tried to persuade you, by threat or intimidation, not to complain, to withdraw your complaint, not to co-operate with the Commissioner, not to give information, or to give false or misleading information
      `did the person have a reasonable excuse` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person had a reasonable excuse (as a court would find)
      `did the person have another, reasonable ground` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person had another ground for the conduct, and that ground is a reasonable one (as a court would find)
GIVETH A `Offence answer`
`Was the intimidation an offence` MEANS
    `s 67 -- intimidation` `was I threatened or intimidated over my complaint` `did the person have a reasonable excuse` `did the person have another, reasonable ground`

@export Was it an offence to take a reprisal against me (for example refusing to employ me, dismissing me, or penalising or discriminating against me at work) because I complained or co-operated with the Commissioner (Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) s 68)? Yes, with a fine of up to 100 penalty units, if your complaint or co-operation was a significant factor, unless the person proves another, reasonable ground and that they had taken a significant step towards the action before acting on your complaint.
GIVEN `was a reprisal taken, attempted or conspired against me` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether someone took, attempted or conspired to take a reprisal against you (a detriment, such as refusing to employ you, dismissing you, or penalising or discriminating against you at work)
      `was my complaint or co-operation a significant factor` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether your complaint (made or possible), your co-operation with the Commissioner, or your giving information, or the belief of any of these, was a significant factor in it
      `did the person have another, reasonable ground` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person proves another ground for the conduct, and that it is a reasonable one
      `had the person taken a significant step towards it beforehand` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person proves they had taken a significant step towards the conduct before acting on your complaint or co-operation
GIVETH A `Offence answer`
`Was the reprisal an offence` MEANS
    `s 68 -- reprisal` `was a reprisal taken, attempted or conspired against me` `was my complaint or co-operation a significant factor` `did the person have another, reasonable ground` `had the person taken a significant step towards it beforehand`

@export By when must a prosecution for an offence against the Health Complaints Act 1995 (Tas) (such as intimidation or a reprisal against me) be started (s 71)? Within 6 months after the offence, or within 6 months after it came to the Commissioner's knowledge, whichever is later. The answer is the last day.
GIVEN `when was the offence committed` IS A DATE @desc The day the offence was committed
      `when did the Commissioner learn of it` IS A DATE @desc The day the offence came to the Commissioner's knowledge
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must a prosecution be started` MEANS
    `s 71 -- the last day to start a prosecution` `when was the offence committed` `when did the Commissioner learn of it`
